4 78 60Z\"></path></defs></svg>","mathFallback":false,"description":"JEE Main's 2011 AIEEE 2011 Paper's All Questions with solutions provider ExamSIDE.Com","paperTitle":"AIEEE 2011","nextq":"3mIl7ePHUNR0dez9","prevq":"mkBnXGogUTYq8ycE","questions":[{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","question_id":"KElf6QK1sAhJKW8C","exam":"jee-main","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","languages":["en"],"chapter":"chemical-bonding-and-molecular-structure","year":2011,"paperTitle":"AIEEE 2011","difficulty":"easy","topicName":null,"type":"mcq","examDate":null,"question":{"en":{"content":"The structure of IF<sub>7</sub> is :","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"trigonal bipyramid"},{"identifier":"B","content":"octahedral"},{"identifier":"C","content":"pentagonal bipyramid"},{"identifier":"D","content":"square pyramid"}],"correct_options":["C"],"answer":null,"explanation":"The structure of IF<sub>7</sub> pentagonal bipyramidal having\nsp<sup>3</sup>d<sup>3</sup>\n hybridisation.\n<br><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 320px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/3xmhtm2xjkbg0gzc5/56613c99-0646-4663-9713-a319105263ca/981e9150-aec2-11ea-9207-bfdf7589d371/file-3xmhtm2xjkbg0gzc6-320w.jpg\"><source media=\"(max-width: 500px)\" srcset=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/3xmhtm2xjkbg0gzc5/56613c99-0646-4663-9713-a319105263ca/981e9150-aec2-11ea-9207-bfdf7589d371/file-3xmhtm2xjkbg0gzc6-500w.jpg\"><img src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/3xmhtm2xjkbg0gzc5/56613c99-0646-4663-9713-a319105263ca/981e9150-aec2-11ea-9207-bfdf7589d371/file-3xmhtm2xjkbg0gzc6-680w.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;height: auto;display: block;margin: 0 auto;\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"AIEEE 2011 Chemistry - Chemical Bonding &amp; Molecular Structure Question 242 English Explanation\"></picture>"}},"permalink":"the-structure-of-if7-is-2011-marks-4-kelf6qk1sahjkw8c.htm","timeAllotted":120000,"updated_time":1592197800,"paperId":"aieee-2011","topic":"hybridization-and-vsepr-theory","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"tags":[],"yearKey":"aieee-2011","active":true},{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","question_id":"NlQwrJQxyidRbufT","exam":"jee-main","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","languages":["en"],"chapter":"chemical-bonding-and-molecular-structure","year":2011,"paperTitle":"AIEEE 2011","difficulty":"easy","topicName":null,"type":"mcq","examDate":null,"question":{"en":{"content":"Among the following the maximum covalent character is shown by the compound : ","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"SnCl<sub>2 </sub>"},{"identifier":"B","content":"AlCl<sub>3</sub> "},{"identifier":"C","content":"MgCl<sub>2 </sub>"},{"identifier":"D","content":"FeCl<sub>2</sub>"}],"correct_options":["B"],"answer":null,"explanation":"Charge of cation/Size of cation is called polarising power.\n<br><br><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.509ex\" height=\"1.251ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -471 667 553\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2234\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2234\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> (i) Polarising power <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 778 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"221D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-221D\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> charge of cation\n<br><br>(ii) Polarising power <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.025ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -442 778 453\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"221D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-221D\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> <span style=\"display: inline-block;vertical-align: middle;\">\n     <div style=\"text-align: center;border-bottom: 1px solid black;\">1</div>\n     <div style=\"text-align: center;\">size of cation</div>\n</span>\n<br><br>Here the AlCl<sub>3</sub>\n is satisfying the above two conditions i.e., Al\nis in +3 oxidation state and also has small size. So it has more\ncovalent character.\n"}},"permalink":"among-the-following-the-maximum-covalent-character-is-shown-2011-marks-4-nlqwrjqxyidrbuft.htm","timeAllotted":120000,"updated_time":1592194874,"paperId":"aieee-2011","topic":"ionic-bond","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"tags":[],"yearKey":"aieee-2011","active":true},{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","question_id":"9BvtDiokWWAJbNgs","exam":"jee-main","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","languages":["en"],"chapter":"periodic-table-and-periodicity","year":2011,"paperTitle":"AIEEE 2011","difficulty":"easy","topicName":null,"type":"mcq","examDate":null,"question":{"en":{"content":"Which one of the following order represents the correct sequence of the increasing basic nature of the\ngiven oxides ?","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"MgO &lt; K<sub>2</sub>O &lt; Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> &lt; Na<sub>2</sub>O"},{"identifier":"B","content":"Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> &lt; MgO &lt; Na<sub>2</sub>O &lt; K<sub>2</sub>O"},{"identifier":"C","content":"Na<sub>2</sub>O &lt; K<sub>2</sub>O &lt; MgO &lt; Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>"},{"identifier":"D","content":"K<sub>2</sub>O &lt; Na<sub>2</sub>O &lt; Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> &lt; MgO"}],"correct_options":["B"],"answer":null,"explanation":"You should know that, \n<br><br>(1)<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.133ex\" height=\"0.036ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 0 501 16\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(167,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(334,0)\"></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> In a period, from left to right the acidic strength increases that mean basic nature decreases.\n<br><br>(2)<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.133ex\" height=\"0.036ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 0 501 16\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(167,0)\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mspace\" transform=\"translate(334,0)\"></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> In a group, from top to bottom the basic nature increases that means acidic nature decreases. \n<br><br>Na, Mg,  Al and K are present in periodic table like this : -\n<br><br>\n<table class=\"tg\">\n  <tbody><tr>\n    <th class=\"tg-de48\"></th>\n    <th class=\"tg-de48\">Group 1</th>\n    <th class=\"tg-de48\">Group 2</th>\n    <th class=\"tg-de48\"></th>\n    <th class=\"tg-ujoh\">Group 13</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td class=\"tg-de48\">Period 3</td>\n    <td class=\"tg-wv9z\">Na</td>\n    <td class=\"tg-wv9z\">Mg</td>\n    <td class=\"tg-wv9z\">.....</td>\n    <td class=\"tg-wrg0\">Al</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td class=\"tg-de48\">Period 4</td>\n    <td class=\"tg-wv9z\">K</td>\n    <td class=\"tg-wv9z\"></td>\n    <td class=\"tg-wv9z\"></td>\n    <td class=\"tg-wrg0\"></td>\n  </tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<br><br>So, you can see Na,  Mg and Al are present in same period so the order of basic nature among Na, Mg and Al is Na &gt; Mg &gt; Al.\n<br><br>And Na and K are from same group so basic nature of K &gt; Na.\n<br><br>So the correct order of oxides \n<br><br>Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> &lt; MgO &lt; Na<sub>2</sub>O &lt; K<sub>2</sub>O "}},"permalink":"which-one-of-the-following-order-represents-the-correct-sequ-2011-marks-4-9bvtdiokwwajbngs.htm","updated_time":1543505362,"timeAllotted":120000,"paperId":"aieee-2011","topic":"nature-of-oxide","isOutOfSyllabus":false,"isBonus":false,"tags":["sequence_ordering"],"yearKey":"aieee-2011","active":true},{"country":"in","examGroup":"jee","question_id":"xOzKpr8XmbTNXmji","exam":"jee-main","marks":4,"negMarks":1,"subjectGroup":null,"subject":"chemistry","chapterGroup":"physical-chemistry","languages":["en"],"chapter":"structure-of-atom","year":2011,"paperTitle":"AIEEE 2011","difficulty":"medium","topicName":null,"type":"mcq","examDate":null,"question":{"en":{"content":"A gas absorbs a photon of 355 nm and emits at two wavelengths. If one of the emissions is at\n 680 nm, the other is at :","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"325 nm "},{"identifier":"B","content":"743 nm "},{"identifier":"C","content":"518 nm"},{"identifier":"D","content":"1035 nm"}],"correct_options":["B"],"answer":null,"explanation":"Here a photon is divided into two photons of different wavelength.\n<br><br>According to the law of conservation of energy,\n<br><br>Energy of photon before dividing into two parts = Energy of first photon + Energy of second photon\n<br><br><mjx-container class=\"M